The vocab/presentation DSL wraps the [[Reveal.js plugin|$:/plugins/sukima/reveal-js]] in a Fountain-flavoured screenplay vocabulary. Activate via `type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki;vocab=Presentation`. Each deck opens with a `PRESENTATION` marker and closes with `FADE OUT.`; slides start with `CUT TO:`, `ANIMATE TO:`, or `FADE IN:`.

For slide-level features that need per-slide configuration (backgrounds, vertical sub-slides, code blocks), drop down to the raw `<$slide>` / `<$slidecodeblock>` widgets inside the deck. The markers handle the rhythm, the widgets handle the details.

!! 1. Basic deck

PRESENTATION

FADE IN:
* Welcome to ''Reveal.js''
* Embedded in TiddlyWiki

CUT TO:
* The second slide

CUT TO:
* The third slide
* Have fun!

FADE OUT.

!! 2. Slide-starter variants

The three slide markers all emit `<section>` and are stylistically interchangeable. Pick whichever fits the rhythm of your deck.

|! Marker |! Intended feel |
|`FADE IN:` |Opening / soft entrance |
|`CUT TO:` |Sharp transition to next topic |
|`ANIMATE TO:` |Build up bullets one at a time |

!! 3. Bullet animations

Reveal.js animates bullet points by re-rendering the same slide with different `.gray` classes. Use `ANIMATE TO:` for each step.

PRESENTATION

ANIMATE TO:
* First Bullet Point
*.gray Second Bullet Point

ANIMATE TO:
* First Bullet Point
* Second Bullet Point

ANIMATE TO:
*.gray First Bullet Point
*.gray Second Bullet Point
* Third Bullet Point

FADE OUT.

!! 4. Vertical sub-slides

Reveal.js lets a horizontal slide own a column of vertical sub-slides (press Down to descend). The DSL doesn't have a marker for this — `CUT TO:` ends at the first blank line, so a body cannot contain further `<$slide>` widgets cleanly. Use a raw `<$slide>` widget for both the outer slide and its inner sub-slides:

PRESENTATION

CUT TO:
* The slide before the vertical column

<$slide>

This horizontal slide has two vertical sub-slides.

<$slide>Sub-slide one (press Down)</$slide>

<$slide>Sub-slide two</$slide>

</$slide>

CUT TO:
* Back on the main horizontal track

FADE OUT.

The outer `<$slide>` contains the two inner `<$slide>` widgets in its body. Reveal.js renders this as a vertical column reachable by pressing Down from the first slide.

!! 5. Slide backgrounds

Per-slide background uses Reveal.js's `data-background-*` attributes. The widget converts camelCase to `data-*`, so `backgroundColor="aquamarine"` becomes `data-background-color="aquamarine"`:

PRESENTATION

<$slide backgroundColor="aquamarine">

* Aquamarine background

</$slide>

<$slide backgroundColor="#222">

* Dark background

</$slide>

<$slide backgroundImage="https://picsum.photos/seed/picsum/800/600">

* Background image

</$slide>

FADE OUT.

!! 6. Code blocks

A slide can hold a code block via a plain wikitext fence. Reveal.js auto-highlights the contents based on the fence language tag. The slide containing the fence must be a raw `<$slide>` (a `CUT TO:` marker ends at the first blank line, so it can't hold a multi-line code fence cleanly).

PRESENTATION

FADE IN:
* Code blocks in Reveal.js
* Press right arrow to step through

<$slide>

JavaScript example:

```js
const greet = function (name) {
	console.log('Hello, ' + name);
};
greet('TiddlyWiki');
```

</$slide>

<$slide>

Same code, Python flavour:

```python
def greet(name):
	print(f'Hello, {name}')

greet('TiddlyWiki')
```

</$slide>

CUT TO:
* That's all
* Press left arrow to step back

FADE OUT.

For deeper Reveal.js features (line-number animation between slides, fragment-by-fragment reveal of code), use the plugin's own `<$slidecodeblock>` widget. See the [[Reveal.js plugin readme|$:/plugins/sukima/reveal-js/readme]] for its attributes.

!! 7. Continuous loop with auto-advance

`PRESENTATION LOOP` is a preset variant that bakes `autoSlide="5000"` and `loop="yes"` into the deck. Useful for kiosks or background displays.

PRESENTATION LOOP

CUT TO:
Slide A advances automatically after 5 seconds...

CUT TO:
...slide B follows...

CUT TO:
...then the deck loops back to A.

FADE OUT.

!! 8. Built from a filter

For decks where the slide content lives in its own tiddlers, mix `PRESENTATION` with a `<$list>` widget inside. Each iteration emits one `<$slide>`. The three tiddlers `demo-slide-welcome`, `demo-slide-features`, `demo-slide-end` (tagged `demo-slide` with a `color` field) drive the deck below.

PRESENTATION

<$list filter="[tag[demo-slide]sort[title]]">

<$slide tiddlerTitle={{!!title}} backgroundColor={{!!color}}>

<$transclude mode="block" />

</$slide>

</$list>

FADE OUT.

`tiddlerTitle` is special: it becomes `data-tiddler-title` on the section element so your CSS can target individual slides. Replace the filter to drive the deck from any tag, sort order, or computed list.

!! 9. Custom deck height

The DSL does not bake a height variant. Default is 400px. For taller decks, use a raw `<$presentation>` widget with `$height` set to any CSS length (no unit = px):

```
<$presentation $height="600">
<$slide>This deck is 600px tall.</$slide>
<$slide>Useful for slides with more vertical content.</$slide>
</$presentation>
```

Inside the raw widget you can still mix `<$slide>`, `<$slidecodeblock>`, and any other Reveal.js construct. The DSL markers (CUT TO: etc.) only work inside a marker-driven deck (`PRESENTATION` or `PRESENTATION LOOP`).

!! Markers in this vocabulary

|! Marker |! Open literal |! Notes |
|Presentation |`PRESENTATION` |Opens a deck. Terminator: `FADE OUT.` |
|Presentation Loop |`PRESENTATION LOOP` |Same shape as `PRESENTATION` plus `autoSlide=5000`, `loop=yes` |
|Cut to |`CUT TO:` |A plain slide |
|Animate to |`ANIMATE TO:` |Same shape as `CUT TO:`; use to imply bullet build-up |
|Fade in |`FADE IN:` |Same shape as `CUT TO:`; use as the opening slide |

The full marker tiddlers live under `vocab/presentation/*` and can be opened from this list to inspect every field. See <<tag $:/tags/CustomMarkup/Marker>> for the registry tag.

!! Known caveats

The Reveal.js library initialises once per tiddler. Slides added or removed from a deck via the wiki tools won't appear until the tiddler is closed and reopened (the README on the [[Reveal.js plugin|$:/plugins/sukima/reveal-js]] covers this in detail).

